WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:05.322
When reading a CTE query, do not read top to bottom as one giant statement.

00:00:05.322 --> 00:00:08.297
First understand the CTE by itself.

00:00:08.297 --> 00:00:11.692
Then ask what the final query does with that stage.

00:00:11.692 --> 00:00:14.312
Here the CTE filters patients.

00:00:14.312 --> 00:00:19.522
The final query joins admissions and aggregates.

00:00:19.522 --> 00:00:22.657
This staged reading style is one reason CTEs improve clarity.

00:00:22.657 --> 00:00:29.452
A CTE gives a name to an intermediate result so a complex query can be read in stages.

00:00:29.452 --> 00:00:36.917
Use it to clarify grain changes and major transformations, not simply to break every two lines into a

00:00:36.917 --> 00:00:43.600
new block. A useful CTE name describes what the rows represent, such as patient_totals or ranked_visits.
